{
  "id": "present-simple-i-you",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "Every day",
  "title": "Present simple: I, you, we, they",
  "tagline": "Your everyday life: routines, likes and facts with I, you, we and they.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Speaking Part 1 — questions about your daily routine",
    "Reading Part 3 — a longer text about someone's life"
  ],
  "why": "Present simple is how you talk about your everyday life: what you do every day, what you like, and what is true. In Speaking Part 1 the examiner asks about your routine and your free time — and you always answer with the present simple.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "On the school bus, Sofia asks Mateo about his morning. \"I <b>get up</b> at half past six,\" he says. \"I <b>don't have</b> breakfast at school — I <b>eat</b> at home. <b>Do</b> you <b>walk</b> to the bus stop?\" \"No, I <b>don't</b>. My mum and I <b>drive</b>!\" says Sofia.",
    "ask": "Find the negative with don't, the question with Do, and the short answer. Which verbs have no -s?"
  },
